export const PAIRS: [string, string][] = [
  ["DPDP Rules 2025: a clear-eyed analysis of industry implications", "DPDP Rules 2025: a clear analysis of what they mean for industry"],
  ["Detecting unauthorised use of personal data", "Finding use of personal data that was not allowed"],
  // ---- consent-manager-role ----
  [
    "If you are deciding whether your product needs to build consent infrastructure from scratch or hand the job to a regulated intermediary, the Consent Manager is the piece of the DPDP framework built specifically for that question. The Act defines it as a registered entity that acts as a single point of contact, enabling a Data Principal to give, manage, review and withdraw consent through an accessible, transparent and interoperable platform.",
    "If you are deciding whether to build your own permission system or use a regulated service, the DPDP framework offers a Consent Manager. The Act defines this as a registered organisation that provides one contact point. Its platform must be easy to access, clear, and able to work with other systems. It lets a person give, manage, review, and withdraw permission for use of their data.",
  ],
  ["The Account Aggregator blueprint", "The Account Aggregator model"],
  [
    "A Consent Manager relays consent requests from a Data Fiduciary to a Data Principal, records both the consent given and the notice that preceded it, and routes that consent to the right fiduciary without being able to read the contents passing through it. The architecture borrows directly from India's Account Aggregator system in financial services, relying on data-blind routing. The Data Principal gets one dashboard to review and withdraw consent across every connected app, rather than chasing settings pages across a dozen different platforms.",
    "A Consent Manager sends permission requests from the organisation responsible for the data to the person the data is about. It records both the permission and the notice shown before it. It sends the permission to the right organisation but cannot read the data passing through. This design comes from India's Account Aggregator system for financial services and routes data without viewing it. The person gets one dashboard to review or withdraw permission for every connected app.",
  ],
  ["The high bar for registration", "Strict registration rules"],
  [
    "Rule 4 and the First Schedule of the DPDP Rules, 2025 set a deliberately high registration bar, since Consent Managers will be trusted with consent records across an entire ecosystem.",
    "Rule 4 and the First Schedule of the DPDP Rules, 2025 set strict registration rules. This is because Consent Managers will hold permission records across a whole network of services.",
  ],
  ["Incorporation as a company in India only; no foreign entities or unincorporated platforms.", "It must be set up as a company in India. Foreign organisations and platforms that are not companies cannot register."],
  ["A minimum net worth of two crore rupees, with capital structure commensurate with the role.", "It must have a net worth of at least two crore rupees and enough suitable capital for the role."],
  ["Demonstrated technical, operational and financial capacity to run the platform reliably.", "It must prove it has the technical, practical, and financial ability to run the platform reliably."],
  ["Leadership with a reputation for fairness, and governing documents that hard-wire fiduciary duties.", "Its leaders must be known for fairness. Its governing documents must formally require duties to the people it serves."],
  ["An independent certification confirming the platform meets the data-protection standards the Board publishes.", "An independent certificate must confirm that the platform meets the data-protection standards published by the Board."],
  ["Do you actually need one?", "Must you use one?"],
  [
    "No. The Rules do not compel a Data Fiduciary to route consent through a Consent Manager. Collecting consent directly is entirely fine if you can independently meet the same notice, withdrawal and record-keeping bar. Where a Consent Manager earns its place is in ecosystems with frequent cross-entity sharing, like healthcare networks or public benefit schemes, where a common, auditable consent layer beats every fiduciary quietly building its own version of the same thing. For most product teams, the near-term task is simply making sure your own direct consent flow already meets the standard.",
    "No. The Rules do not force an organisation responsible for data to use a Consent Manager. You may collect permission directly if you meet the same rules for notices, withdrawal, and records. A Consent Manager can help networks that often share data between organisations, such as healthcare networks or public benefit schemes. One shared permission system that can be audited may work better than separate systems. For now, most product teams should make sure their own permission process meets the standard.",
  ],

  // ---- cybersecurity-role-dpdp-act ----
  [
    "Cybersecurity plays a central but carefully defined role in India's DPDP framework. The Act is not a cybersecurity law in the narrow sense; it is a personal-data protection law. But its core outcomes - lawful processing, purpose limitation, data minimisation, rights fulfilment, breach notification and accountability - only become real when security and data-governance controls are embedded into daily operations.",
    "Cybersecurity has an important but specific role in India's DPDP framework. The Act is a personal-data protection law, not only a cybersecurity law. But its main goals work only when security and data controls are part of daily work. These goals include using data legally, using it only for the stated reason, collecting only what is needed, supporting people's rights, reporting data leaks, and taking responsibility.",
  ],
  [
    "For Boards and CXOs, the message is direct: DPDP is not just a legal compliance project. It is an operating model for digital trust. For practitioners, that means mapping personal data, controlling access, securing processors, logging activity, detecting misuse, responding to incidents and retaining enough evidence to prove what happened.",
    "For Boards and senior executives, DPDP is not just a project to follow the law. It is a way to build trust in digital services. Teams must map personal data, control access, secure companies that handle data for them, keep activity logs, find misuse, respond to incidents, and keep enough proof to show what happened.",
  ],
  ["The core thesis", "The main point"],
  [
    "Under DPDP, cybersecurity is the control layer that allows a Data Fiduciary to protect personal data in its possession or control, including processing performed through Data Processors. Reasonable security safeguards are not an isolated checkbox. They support consent, purpose limitation, data minimisation, retention, grievance handling and breach response.",
    "Under DPDP, cybersecurity helps an organisation protect personal data it holds or controls. This includes data handled by another company for it. Reasonable security protections are not a single checkbox. They support permission, use only for the stated reason, collecting only what is needed, keeping data for the right time, handling complaints, and responding to data leaks.",
  ],
  [
    "A weak security programme turns privacy into a promise nobody can verify. If an organisation cannot identify where personal data sits, who touched it, why it moved, whether it was exposed and how it can be corrected or erased, then its DPDP readiness is mostly paper.",
    "Weak security makes privacy a promise that cannot be proved. An organisation must know where personal data is, who used it, why it moved, whether it was exposed, and how to correct or delete it. Without this knowledge, its DPDP readiness exists mostly on paper.",
  ],
  ["Lifecycle control map", "Controls for each stage of data use"],
  ["DPDP objective", "DPDP goal"],
  ["Practical cybersecurity controls", "Security controls to use"],
  ["Discover and classify personal data", "Find and label personal data"],
  ["Data inventories, data-flow maps, automated classification, KYC/document tagging, processor and sub-processor inventories.", "Lists of data, maps of where data moves, automatic labels, KYC and document tags, and lists of every company and subcontractor that handles data."],
  ["The Act regulates processing across the full lifecycle, from collection and storage to sharing, erasure and destruction. You cannot protect or delete what you cannot locate.", "The Act covers data through its whole life, from collection and storage to sharing, deletion, and destruction. You cannot protect or delete data if you cannot find it."],
  ["Collect only what is needed", "Collect only the data you need"],
  ["Privacy-by-design intake forms, field minimisation, purpose-bound consent, consent logs, just-in-time notices and permission controls.", "Forms designed for privacy, fewer data fields, permission tied to a stated reason, permission logs, notices shown when needed, and access controls."],
  ["Consent and notice are tied to specified purposes. Cybersecurity and product design must prevent quiet over-collection.", "Permission and notices are tied to exact stated reasons. Security and product design must stop hidden collection of extra data."],
  ["Control access and prevent misuse", "Control access and stop misuse"],
  ["IAM, MFA, RBAC or ABAC, privileged access management, conditional access, just-in-time admin, segregation of duties and periodic access reviews.", "Identity and access management (IAM), multi-factor authentication (MFA), role- or attribute-based access, controls for powerful accounts, access based on conditions, temporary admin access, separated duties, and regular access reviews."],
  ["Most privacy failures begin as excessive access, stale privileges or compromised credentials.", "Most privacy failures begin with too much access, old permissions that were not removed, or stolen login details."],
  ["Secure storage, processing and transfer", "Secure data when stored, used, or sent"],
  ["Encryption at rest and in transit, tokenisation, masking, key management, secure APIs, secrets vaulting, secure deletion and processor controls.", "Encryption for stored and moving data, tokenisation, masking, encryption-key controls, secure APIs, protected passwords and keys, secure deletion, and controls for companies that handle data."],
  ["Technical controls preserve confidentiality and reduce blast radius when systems fail.", "Technical controls keep data private and limit the harm when systems fail."],
  ["Monitor access and detect misuse", "Watch access and find misuse"],
  ["Central logging, SIEM, DLP, anomaly detection, API monitoring, database activity monitoring and exfiltration alerts.", "Central logs, security monitoring (SIEM), data-loss prevention (DLP), checks for unusual activity, API and database monitoring, and alerts when data may be taken out."],
  ["Breach duties only work if the organisation can detect unauthorised access or disclosure in time.", "Data-leak duties only work if the organisation can find access or sharing that was not allowed in time."],
  ["Maintain audit trails and evidence", "Keep audit records and proof"],
  ["Tamper-resistant logs, time synchronisation, retention, incident records, consent audit trails and chain-of-custody discipline.", "Logs that cannot easily be changed, matching system clocks, required keeping periods, incident records, permission histories, and records of who handled evidence."],
  ["DPDP accountability depends on being able to reconstruct decisions, access and remediation after the fact.", "DPDP responsibility depends on being able to show later what decisions were made, who had access, and how the problem was fixed."],
  ["Enable Data Principal rights", "Support people's data rights"],
  ["Rights portals, identity verification, consent-withdrawal workflows, correction and erasure workflows, processor propagation and grievance tracking.", "Rights request pages, identity checks, ways to withdraw permission, ways to correct and delete data, updates sent to companies handling the data, and complaint tracking."],
  ["Rights under the Act need backend systems that can find, verify, update, restrict or erase data reliably.", "Rights under the Act need systems that can reliably find, check, update, limit, or delete data."],
  ["Breach management under DPDP", "Managing a data leak under DPDP"],
  [
    "A personal-data breach is not only a security incident. It is a governance event. DPDP requires the Data Fiduciary to intimate the Data Protection Board and affected Data Principals in the prescribed form and manner when a personal-data breach occurs. The Rules add operational detail around clear communication, likely consequences, mitigation steps, contact information and follow-up information to the Board.",
    "A personal-data leak is not only a security incident. It also requires management action. When one happens, DPDP requires the organisation responsible for the data to tell the Data Protection Board and affected people in the form and process set by the rules. The Rules also require clear messages about likely effects, steps to reduce harm, contact details, and later information for the Board.",
  ],
  ["Prevent: classify personal data, encrypt or tokenise sensitive fields, enforce least privilege and MFA, secure APIs, test backups and bind processors through strong contractual obligations.", "Prevent: label personal data, encrypt or tokenise sensitive fields, give only the minimum access, use MFA, secure APIs, test backups, and put strong duties in contracts with companies that handle data."],
  ["Detect: run SIEM, DLP, API monitoring and anomaly detection; preserve relevant logs; investigate unusual access, bulk exports and exfiltration signals.", "Detect: use SIEM, DLP, API monitoring, and checks for unusual activity. Keep relevant logs. Investigate unusual access, large exports, and signs that data is being taken out."],
  ["Contain: disable compromised accounts or API keys, isolate workloads, block exfiltration paths, rotate secrets and preserve evidence before systems are overwritten.", "Contain: disable stolen accounts or API keys, separate affected systems, block ways data can be taken out, replace passwords and keys, and save evidence before systems overwrite it."],
  ["Notify: assess whether the DPDP personal-data breach threshold is met, notify affected Data Principals and the Board as required, and separately assess whether CERT-In reporting is triggered for the same cyber incident.", "Notify: decide whether the incident meets the DPDP personal-data leak test. Tell affected people and the Board as required. Separately check whether the same cyber incident must also be reported to CERT-In."],
  ["Remediate: complete root-cause analysis, patch the defect, review access, revisit processor controls, retest safeguards and record lessons learned for the Board, CISO and DPO.", "Fix: find the main cause, repair the fault, review access, check controls for companies handling data, test protections again, and record lessons for the Board, CISO, and DPO."],
  ["Strategic value beyond compliance", "Benefits beyond following the law"],
  ["For Boards, DPDP turns cybersecurity into trust, resilience and accountability governance. The penalty schedule makes failures in safeguards and breach notification financially material, but the reputational issue is often larger: customers, partners and regulators want proof that personal data is handled with discipline.", "For Boards, DPDP makes cybersecurity part of trust, recovery, and responsibility. Failures in security protections and data-leak reporting can bring important financial penalties. Harm to reputation may be even larger. Customers, partners, and authorities want proof that personal data is handled carefully."],
  ["For CISOs and DPOs, the value is operational. A serious DPDP programme improves data inventories, identity governance, vendor contracts, breach response, audit trails and customer commitments. It also aligns security with privacy outcomes instead of leaving privacy trapped in policy documents.", "For CISOs and DPOs, the benefit is practical. A strong DPDP programme improves data lists, identity controls, outside-partner contracts, data-leak response, audit records, and promises to customers. It makes security support real privacy results instead of leaving privacy only in policy documents."],
  ["Beyond firewalls and antivirus", "More than firewalls and antivirus"],
  ["A mature DPDP cybersecurity scope should include privacy engineering for purpose limitation and minimisation; zero-trust access for identity, device posture and per-session authorisation; encryption, masking and tokenisation for confidentiality; logging and monitoring for misuse detection; incident simulations for breach readiness; secure consent systems for proof and withdrawal; AI and data governance for algorithmic processing risks; and human process discipline through SOPs, training and vendor governance.", "A strong DPDP security programme should cover: privacy design that uses data only for the stated reason and collects only what is needed; zero-trust checks for identity, device safety, and each session; encryption, masking, and tokenisation to keep data private; logs and monitoring to find misuse; practice incidents to prepare for data leaks; secure permission systems with proof and withdrawal; controls for AI and algorithm risks; and clear SOPs, training, and outside-partner controls."],
  ["The regulated object is not the network. It is personal data and the rights attached to it. That is why DPDP security has to follow the data across applications, cloud services, vendors, employees, APIs, archives and incident workflows.", "The law does not protect only the network. It protects personal data and the rights linked to it. DPDP security must therefore follow data across apps, cloud services, outside partners, employees, APIs, archives, and incident processes."],
  ["India-specific example: fintech and KYC data", "Indian example: fintech and KYC data"],
  ["Consider an Indian fintech or SaaS platform that supports digital onboarding, KYC, underwriting and loan servicing for a regulated lender. It may handle identity documents, bank details, contact data, income or occupation data, device/session data and repayment records.", "Consider an Indian fintech or SaaS platform that helps a regulated lender sign up customers, complete KYC, assess loans, and manage loans. It may handle identity documents, bank details, contact details, income or job data, device and session data, and repayment records."],
  ["Onboarding should use a clear DPDP notice, collect only data needed for the stated purpose, log consent and avoid unnecessary device permissions.", "Signup should use a clear DPDP notice, collect only data needed for the stated reason, record permission, and avoid device permissions that are not needed."],
  ["KYC documents and recordings should be encrypted, tokenised where possible, restricted to authorised teams and monitored through access logs.", "KYC documents and recordings should be encrypted, tokenised where possible, limited to allowed teams, and watched through access logs."],
  ["Cloud providers, lending-service providers, analytics tools and other processors should be contractually bound to security, breach-cooperation, deletion and audit obligations.", "Contracts should require cloud providers, lending-service providers, analytics tools, and other data handlers to meet duties for security, help with data leaks, deletion, and audits."],
  ["Rights workflows should support access, correction, updating, erasure, withdrawal and grievance handling, while routing erasure through legal-retention checks where another law requires preservation.", "Rights processes should support access, correction, updates, deletion, withdrawal, and complaints. Before deletion, they must check whether another law requires the data to be kept."],
  ["If an API misconfiguration exposes KYC records, the team should revoke keys, disable the endpoint, preserve logs, identify affected Data Principals, notify under DPDP as required and assess parallel CERT-In reporting.", "If a wrongly set up API exposes KYC records, the team should cancel keys, disable the endpoint, save logs, identify affected people, report under DPDP as required, and check whether it must also report to CERT-In."],
  ["Implementation guardrails", "Rules for putting this into practice"],
  ["Do not describe DPDP as only a cybersecurity law. It is a privacy law whose obligations depend heavily on cybersecurity controls.", "Do not call DPDP only a cybersecurity law. It is a privacy law, and many of its duties depend on cybersecurity controls."],
  ["Keep DPDP, DPDP Rules, CERT-In and sectoral obligations separate in internal playbooks. One incident may trigger more than one reporting route.", "Keep duties under DPDP, the DPDP Rules, CERT-In, and industry rules separate in internal plans. One incident may need more than one report."],
  ["Do not publish internal links, private customer material or vendor-confidential implementation notes on public pages.", "Do not put internal links, private customer material, or confidential outside-partner notes on public pages."],
  ["Avoid fear-based claims. Explain the control, the evidence it creates and the risk it reduces.", "Do not use claims based on fear. Explain the control, the proof it creates, and the risk it lowers."],
  ["Make legal review part of the final publication workflow where statutory duties, penalties or timelines are discussed.", "Require a legal review before publishing information about legal duties, penalties, or deadlines."],
  ["Board-ready takeaway", "Main point for the Board"],
  ["Cybersecurity is the DPDP control plane. The Act creates the privacy obligations; the Rules define many operational expectations; and Indian cyber and sectoral regimes can add parallel duties. For Boards, the ask is oversight of risk, accountability, vendors, resilience and customer trust. For CISOs and DPOs, the ask is an integrated privacy-security operating model: know the data, minimise collection, control access, encrypt or tokenise, monitor misuse, retain evidence, respond fast, notify correctly and continuously improve.", "Cybersecurity is how many DPDP controls work. The Act creates privacy duties. The Rules explain many practical expectations. Indian cyber and industry rules may add separate duties. Boards must oversee risk, responsibility, outside partners, recovery, and customer trust. CISOs and DPOs need one privacy and security model: know the data, collect less, control access, encrypt or tokenise, watch for misuse, keep proof, respond quickly, report correctly, and keep improving."],
  ["Under DPDP, cybersecurity is not merely a technical control. It is the operating system of digital trust.", "Under DPDP, cybersecurity is more than a technical control. It is the working base for trust in digital services."],

  // ---- data-localization-transfers ----
  ["If you are choosing between a Mumbai data centre and a Singapore one, the DPDP framework probably matters less to that decision than you would expect, but not zero. Here is what the Act and Rules actually require when personal data crosses a border, and what they deliberately leave open for businesses operating across jurisdictions.", "If you are choosing between a Mumbai data centre and one in Singapore, DPDP may affect the decision less than expected, but it still matters. Here is what the Act and Rules require when personal data goes outside India, and what choices remain open for businesses working in more than one country."],
  ["The negative list approach", "Countries where transfers may be blocked"],
  ["Section 16 of the Act gives the Central Government the power to restrict transfer of personal data to specific countries by notification. Everywhere else, transfer is permitted by default. This is the inverse of the EU's adequacy-list model, which prohibits transfer unless a destination is pre-approved. That mandatory localisation requirement proposed in earlier draft bills was dropped before the final Act, in favour of this much more liberal negative-list approach.", "Section 16 lets the Central Government issue a notice that limits sending personal data to named countries. Sending data everywhere else is allowed by default. This is the opposite of the EU model, which blocks a transfer unless the destination is approved first. Earlier draft bills required data to stay in India. The final Act removed that rule and instead uses a list of countries where transfers are limited."],
  ["Sectoral overrides still apply", "Industry rules still apply"],
  ["Open by default under DPDP does not mean unregulated. Sectoral localisation rules sit on top of DPDP and are explicitly preserved by Section 16(2).", "DPDP allows transfers by default, but transfers still have rules. Section 16(2) keeps separate industry rules that require some data to stay in a certain place."],
  ["The RBI's payment-data localisation requirement continues to apply at full strength.", "The RBI rule that payment data must stay in the required location still applies in full."],
  ["SEBI's data-residency rules for regulated entities remain entirely unaffected.", "SEBI's rules about where regulated organisations keep data are unchanged."],
  ["No restricted-country list has been notified under Section 16 as of writing, so transfer is open to every destination by default for now.", "At the time of writing, no restricted-country list has been issued under Section 16. For now, transfers to every destination are allowed by default."],
  ["Contractual safeguards and liability", "Contract protections and responsibility"],
  ["Rule 15 operationalises Section 16, but it does not introduce Standard Contractual Clauses the way GDPR does. However, Section 8 keeps a Data Fiduciary responsible for what its Data Processor does with personal data, including a processor sitting overseas. Many organisations are layering contractual safeguards in anyway for vendor accountability. You must map where personal data physically sits, get data-processing terms with every overseas vendor that name the DPDP Act, and watch for Section 16 restricted-country notifications that could take effect on the date of publication with little lead time.", "Rule 15 puts Section 16 into practice, but it does not create Standard Contractual Clauses like GDPR. Section 8 still makes the organisation responsible for what a company handling its data does, including one outside India. Many organisations use contract protections to hold outside partners responsible. Map where personal data is physically stored. Agree data-handling terms with every overseas partner that name the DPDP Act. Watch for Section 16 notices about restricted countries, because they may start on the publication date with little warning."],

  // ---- detecting-unauthorised-use ----
  ["You can write the cleanest consent notice in India, but if you cannot tell when someone inside or outside the organisation has touched personal data they should not have, none of that notice actually protects anyone. This is a practical playbook for building that detection capability, aimed at the people who have to make it work: security engineers, DPOs and whoever owns the breach-response decision.", "A clear permission notice does not protect people if you cannot find out when someone inside or outside the organisation uses personal data without permission. This guide explains how to find that misuse. It is for security engineers, DPOs, and the person responsible for deciding how to respond to a data leak."],
  ["Logging as a legal requirement", "Logs required by law"],
  ["Rule 6 sets a floor for security safeguards: encryption, access control, masking or tokenisation, and a minimum retention period for logs. Rule 8(3) extends that retention requirement to at least one year for all processing, not only for incidents already flagged as breaches. If you do not have access logs reaching back twelve months, you cannot reconstruct unauthorised use even after someone raises a concern about it.", "Rule 6 sets minimum security protections: encryption, access control, masking or tokenisation, and a minimum period for keeping logs. Rule 8(3) requires logs to be kept for at least one year for all use of personal data, not only known data leaks. Without twelve months of access logs, you cannot work out what happened when someone reports possible misuse."],
  ["Signals of unauthorised use", "Signs of use without permission"],
  ["Detection is not just about external hacks. Most unauthorised use is an internal permissions issue or a compromised credential. You need to monitor the right signals.", "Finding misuse is not only about outside attacks. Most use without permission comes from a problem with internal access or stolen login details. Watch for the right signs."],
  ["Bulk exports of customer databases or financial records to external drives or personal emails.", "Large exports of customer databases or financial records to outside drives or personal email accounts."],
  ["Off-hours access to sensitive HR or payroll systems that fall outside a role's normal scope.", "Access outside working hours to sensitive HR or payroll systems that the person's job does not normally require."],
  ["API keys or service accounts querying personal data at volumes that exceed normal application behaviour.", "API keys or service accounts reading much more personal data than the app normally uses."],
  ["A sudden spike in Data Principal grievances under Section 13 regarding data they never shared with you.", "A sudden rise in Section 13 complaints from people about data they never gave you."],
  ["Building the 72-hour workflow", "Setting up the 72-hour process"],
  ["Rule 7 does not allow a risk-based threshold before notification kicks in. Every personal data breach has to be reported to the Board within 72 hours. Ambiguity about who decides is the single most common reason organisations miss that deadline.", "Rule 7 does not allow a minimum risk level before reporting is required. Every personal data leak must be reported to the Board within 72 hours. Organisations most often miss this deadline because they have not clearly named who makes the decision."],
  ["Name one specific individual who can authorise a Board notification without waiting for a committee to convene.", "Name one person who can approve a report to the Board without waiting for a committee meeting."],
  ["Treat every Data Processor contract as a real control point, ensuring audit rights and breach-notification clauses have teeth.", "Use every contract with a company that handles data as a real control. Make sure audit rights and data-leak reporting terms can be enforced."],
  ["Run periodic access reviews against least privilege to revoke permissions nobody remembered to remove.", "Regularly review access and remove permissions that people no longer need."],
  ["Document every investigation, even the false alarms, as a documented conclusion of no breach is a far stronger position than no investigation at all.", "Record every investigation, including false alarms. A written finding that no data leak happened is much stronger than having no investigation."],
  ["Detection is the underlying muscle that makes every other DPDP obligation, from the 72-hour breach clock to grievance redressal, actually achievable on time.", "Finding misuse is what makes other DPDP duties possible on time, from reporting a data leak within 72 hours to handling complaints."],

  // ---- dpdpa-ai-systems ----
  ["The DPDP Act does not use the words artificial intelligence anywhere in its text. That does not mean it is silent on what your model can do with personal data. For AI and product teams, the relevant obligations were already there; they just were not written with a neural network in mind.", "The DPDP Act does not use the words artificial intelligence. But it still controls what an AI model can do with personal data. The duties already apply to AI and product teams, even though they were not written specifically for neural networks."],
  ["The training data dilemma", "The problem with training data"],
  ["Every input that contains personal data needs a basis under Section 4: consent under Section 6, or one of the legitimate uses under Section 7. The often-assumed shortcut, that scraped public web data is automatically free to use, is narrower than it looks. Section 3(c)(ii) excludes from the Act personal data that the individual has voluntarily made public, not personal data that is simply discoverable online. Where voluntarily public ends and merely accessible begins is one of the most actively contested points in current practice, and industry bodies are still seeking clearer carve-outs for AI training.", "Every AI input containing personal data needs a legal reason under Section 4. This must be permission under Section 6 or a legitimate use under Section 7. Public web data collected by scraping is not automatically free to use. Section 3(c)(ii) excludes only personal data that the person chose to make public, not all data that can be found online. The line between data made public by choice and data that is only accessible is still strongly disputed. Industry groups are still asking for clearer exceptions for AI training."],
  ["Purpose limitation and model drift", "Using data for a new AI purpose"],
  ["Consent under Section 6 is tied to a specified purpose. Data collected for customer support, account creation or transaction processing does not automatically carry a licence to fine-tune a model later. That is a new purpose, and a new purpose usually means fresh notice and consent. This is the provision most likely to catch out product teams who quietly repurpose old datasets for new AI features without checking the original consent boundaries.", "Permission under Section 6 is tied to an exact stated reason. Data collected for customer support, account creation, or transactions is not automatically allowed for later fine-tuning of a model. That is a new reason for use and usually needs a new notice and fresh permission. Product teams may break this rule if they quietly reuse old datasets for new AI features without checking the original permission."],
  ["Algorithmic due diligence", "Required checks for algorithms"],
  ["Rule 13(3) requires Significant Data Fiduciaries to exercise due diligence confirming that algorithmic software used in hosting, displaying, modifying or sharing personal data does not pose a risk to Data Principal rights. It is not a model-auditing regime in the style of the EU's AI-specific rules, but it is the clearest hook anywhere in the framework for scrutinising what an algorithm does with personal data, rather than only whether the data was collected lawfully in the first place. Map your datasets, treat repurposing as a new activity, and build output review for models that could infer personal data.", "Rule 13(3) requires Significant Data Fiduciaries to carefully check that software using algorithms to host, show, change, or share personal data does not risk people's rights. This is not a full model-audit system like the EU's AI rules. But it is the clearest DPDP rule for checking what an algorithm does with personal data, not only whether the data was first collected legally. Map your datasets. Treat reuse for a new reason as a new activity. Review outputs from models that could work out personal data."],

  // ---- chatgpt-generative-ai-risks ----
  ["Pasting a customer's complaint email into a chatbot to draft a reply feels like a private moment between you and a tool. Under the DPDP Act, it is a disclosure of personal data to a third party, and that carries real consequences for whoever made the call to paste it. Shadow AI is currently the largest blind spot in corporate data protection.", "Pasting a customer's complaint email into a chatbot may feel private. Under the DPDP Act, it shares personal data with an outside party. The person who chose to paste it may face real consequences. Unapproved AI use is now the largest gap in many companies' data protection."],
  ["Prompts as personal data disclosures", "Prompts that share personal data"],
  ["When a generative AI tool is contracted by your organisation, it is typically acting as a Data Processor on your behalf. Pasting personal data into it for a purpose the original notice and consent under Section 6 never covered raises the same purpose-creep risk as repurposing a dataset for model training. The convenience of a chat window does not exempt it from the basic question of what basis you have to process that data in the first place.", "When your organisation contracts a generative AI tool, the tool usually acts as a company handling data for you. If staff paste personal data into it for a reason not covered by the original notice and permission under Section 6, the data is being used for a new purpose. The same risk applies when a dataset is reused for model training. A chat window does not remove the need for a legal reason to use the data."],
  ["The Data Processor trap", "The risk from a company handling your data"],
  ["Many consumer-grade generative AI tools retain prompts to improve their own models by default, and a number of them run on infrastructure outside India. Section 8 keeps a Data Fiduciary responsible for its Data Processor's conduct, so an organisation that hands customer data to a tool without checking its retention and training settings carries that risk regardless of where the underlying server sits. Section 16 permits this kind of cross-border flow by default, but the responsibility for the data's security travels with it.", "Many consumer generative AI tools keep prompts by default to improve their models. Some run on systems outside India. Under Section 8, the organisation remains responsible for what the company handling its data does. If it gives customer data to a tool without checking how long prompts are kept or used for training, it carries the risk wherever the server is. Section 16 allows this transfer outside India by default, but responsibility for security goes with the data."],
  ["Securing the generative workflow", "Making generative AI use safer"],
  ["A workable policy does not have to be a total ban on AI tools, but it requires strict operational guardrails.", "A practical policy does not have to ban all AI tools, but it needs strict rules for how they are used."],
  ["Classify what counts as personal data before anyone pastes it into a tool: names, identifiers, health or financial detail.", "Clearly label personal data before anyone pastes it into a tool. This includes names, identity details, health data, and financial details."],
  ["Default to enterprise tiers that contractually exclude prompts from training data, over free consumer tiers that do not.", "Prefer business versions whose contracts keep prompts out of training data. Do not default to free consumer versions that use prompts for training."],
  ["Redact or tokenise identifiers where the task genuinely does not need them.", "Remove or tokenise identity details when the task does not truly need them."],
  ["Put generative AI vendors through the same Data Processor due diligence you would apply to any other cloud vendor.", "Check generative AI providers as carefully as any other cloud company that handles your data."],
  ["The risk in generative AI tools is rarely the model itself. It is the personal data an employee feeds it without thinking about where that data was supposed to stop.", "The main risk in generative AI tools is rarely the model. It is personal data that an employee enters without checking where the data is allowed to go."],

  // ---- dpdpa-small-businesses ----
  ["There is no small-business carve-out in the Digital Personal Data Protection Act today, and that surprises almost every founder who hears it for the first time. Here is what actually applies if you run a small operation, and where the genuine relief sits amidst the compliance noise.", "Today, the Digital Personal Data Protection Act has no general exception for small businesses. This surprises many founders. Here is what applies to a small operation and what limited relief the law may provide."],
  ["The startup exemption myth", "The truth about a startup exception"],
  ["Section 17(3) lets the Central Government notify certain Data Fiduciaries, including startups, as exempt from specific obligations like the Section 5 notice requirement or parts of Section 8. As things stand, no such notification has been issued, so this relief is not yet operative. Building a compliance plan around a hypothetical future exemption is a gamble rather than a strategy. Every business processing personal data in India today, regardless of size or revenue, has to comply with the Act as it currently stands.", "Section 17(3) lets the Central Government issue a notice that frees certain Data Fiduciaries, including startups, from named duties. These may include the Section 5 notice duty or parts of Section 8. No such notice has been issued, so this relief does not apply yet. Do not base a plan on an exception that may happen later. Today, every business using personal data in India must follow the Act, whatever its size or income."],
  ["Proportionate safeguards", "Protections that fit the business"],
  ["What that means in practice is proportionate. A five-person shop with a customer spreadsheet does not need an enterprise security operations centre, but it does need basic access control, a backup plan, and a lawful basis for every category of personal data it collects. Most small operations will not be designated a Significant Data Fiduciary under Section 10, meaning you will avoid the heavy Data Protection Impact Assessment and audit obligations reserved for high-volume, high-risk organisations.", "In practice, protections should fit the size and risk of the business. A five-person shop with a customer spreadsheet does not need a large security operations centre. It does need basic access controls, a backup plan, and a legal reason for every type of personal data it collects. Most small operations will not be named a Significant Data Fiduciary under Section 10. They will not have the Data Protection Impact Assessment and audit duties kept for organisations with large amounts of data and high risk."],
  ["The minimal viable compliance stack", "The basic steps for following the law"],
  ["You do not need a massive legal department to get the foundations right. Focus on these practical steps.", "You do not need a large legal team to get the basics right. Focus on these steps."],
  ["List every place personal data enters your business: signup forms, payment gateways, support tickets, vendor tools.", "List every place personal data enters your business, including signup forms, payment services, support tickets, and outside tools."],
  ["Write one standalone notice that actually describes what you collect and why, in language a customer would understand.", "Write a separate notice that clearly says what you collect and why, in words customers can understand."],
  ["Set up one inbox or contact point for grievances and consent withdrawal requests under Section 13.", "Set up one email inbox or contact point for complaints and requests to withdraw permission under Section 13."],
  ["Check your vendors. Section 8 makes you liable for your Data Processors, so ensure your SaaS tools are DPDP-compliant.", "Check outside partners. Section 8 makes you responsible for companies that handle data for you, so make sure your SaaS tools follow DPDP."],
  ["Write down, even briefly, what you would do in the first 72 hours of a suspected breach to meet the Rule 7 clock.", "Write a short plan for what you will do in the first 72 hours after a suspected data leak to meet the Rule 7 deadline."],
  ["None of this requires a massive budget. It requires deciding, once, what data you actually need and being honest with yourself about where it goes afterwards.", "This does not require a large budget. Decide what data you truly need and clearly track where it goes later."],

  // ---- right-to-be-forgotten ----
  ["Ask whether India has a right to be forgotten and the honest answer is: not by that name, but something narrower and more specific exists, and it is worth knowing exactly what it covers before you go looking for it.", "India does not have a right called the right to be forgotten. It has a narrower, more specific right. You should know exactly what it covers before trying to use it."],
  ["The mechanics of Section 12", "How Section 12 works"],
  ["Section 12 gives a Data Principal the right to request correction, completion, updating or erasure of personal data held by a Data Fiduciary. This is a right exercised against a specific organisation holding your data, like your bank or your e-commerce account. It is not a general right to disappear from the internet or compel a search engine to delist a webpage about you, which is closer to what the European right to be forgotten actually does. India's version is deliberately narrower and more transactional.", "Section 12 lets a person ask an organisation holding their data to correct, complete, update, or delete it. You use this right with a specific organisation, such as your bank or an online shop. It does not give a general right to disappear from the internet or force a search engine to remove a webpage from results. The European right to be forgotten is closer to that. India's right is intentionally narrower and applies to specific dealings."],
  ["The retention floor conflict", "When data must still be kept"],
  ["Erasure under DPDP is not purely something you have to ask for. Section 8(7) places an independent duty on the Data Fiduciary to erase personal data once consent is withdrawn, or once the specified purpose is no longer being served. However, a Data Fiduciary can decline an erasure request where retention is necessary to comply with another law. Rule 8 also sets default retention windows for some sectors, and a separate floor requires every Data Fiduciary to keep personal data and processing logs for at least one year before erasure. Erasure rights sit on top of these retention floors, not in place of them.", "Under DPDP, deletion does not always require a request. Section 8(7) separately requires the organisation to delete personal data when permission is withdrawn or the stated reason for using it has ended. But the organisation may refuse deletion if another law requires it to keep the data. Rule 8 also sets standard keeping periods for some sectors. A separate minimum requires every Data Fiduciary to keep personal data and use logs for at least one year before deletion. The right to deletion must follow these minimum keeping rules; it does not replace them."],
  ["Handling the erasure workflow", "Handling a deletion request"],
  ["For DPOs and engineering teams, building the backend to support this right requires clear data mapping.", "DPOs and engineering teams need a clear map of the data to build systems that support this right."],
  ["Identify the specific Data Fiduciary holding the data and use the consent-withdrawal or grievance link its notice is required to provide.", "Identify the exact organisation holding the data. Use the link in its notice for withdrawing permission or making a complaint."],
  ["State exactly what you want corrected, completed or erased, rather than a general request to delete everything.", "Say exactly what you want corrected, completed, or deleted. Do not make only a general request to delete everything."],
  ["Expect a response within the 90-day window the DPDP Rules set for Data Fiduciaries to resolve this kind of request.", "The DPDP Rules give Data Fiduciaries a 90-day period to resolve this kind of request."],
  ["If it goes unresolved, Section 13 gives you a path to raise a formal grievance with the Data Protection Board.", "If it is not resolved, Section 13 lets you make a formal complaint to the Data Protection Board."],
  ["Call it erasure rather than forgetting, and the right makes more sense for what it actually is: a tool for cleaning up your own data trail with specific organisations, not a single switch that wipes your digital footprint everywhere at once.", "Think of this as deletion, not forgetting. It lets you clean up your data with specific organisations. It is not one switch that removes your digital record everywhere."],

  // ---- privacy-in-india-history ----
  ["Privacy in India has had an unusually long and uneven path to becoming a right anyone could actually rely on, and the story of how it got there says almost as much about Indian governance as the law itself does.", "Privacy took a long and uneven path to become a right that people in India could rely on. That history also shows how India makes and puts laws into practice."],
  ["The Puttaswamy watershed", "The major Puttaswamy decision"],
  ["For decades, Indian courts circled the question of whether privacy was protected by the Constitution at all, without a definitive answer for the country as a whole. Earlier judgments found pieces of a privacy interest tucked inside other rights, but no single ruling settled the matter outright. That uncertainty ended in August 2017, when a nine-judge bench of the Supreme Court in Justice K.S. Puttaswamy versus Union of India unanimously held that privacy is a fundamental right protected under Article 21 of the Constitution. The judgment explicitly noted that a right of this significance needed legislative backing, effectively setting the agenda for the decade that followed.", "For decades, Indian courts did not give one final answer on whether the Constitution protected privacy across the country. Earlier decisions found parts of privacy within other rights, but none settled the whole question. In August 2017, a nine-judge bench of the Supreme Court ended the uncertainty. In Justice K.S. Puttaswamy versus Union of India, all nine judges held that privacy is a fundamental right under Article 21 of the Constitution. The judgment clearly said that such an important right needed support from a law. This shaped the work that followed."],
  ["The false starts", "Earlier attempts that did not become law"],
  ["The path from Puttaswamy to an actual statute was not quick. A committee led by Justice B.N. Srikrishna proposed a draft data protection bill in 2018, a revised version was introduced in Parliament in 2019, and it was withdrawn in 2022 after extended committee review and public feedback. The debate over data localisation, state exemptions and corporate compliance costs slowed the process, leaving citizens with a constitutional guarantee but no statutory mechanism to enforce it against private companies.", "A law did not quickly follow Puttaswamy. A committee led by Justice B.N. Srikrishna proposed a draft data protection bill in 2018. A changed bill went to Parliament in 2019. It was withdrawn in 2022 after a long committee review and public comments. Debate about keeping data in India, exceptions for the state, and business costs slowed the process. People had a right under the Constitution but no process in a law to enforce it against private companies."],
  ["The 2023 Act and 2025 Rules", "The Act in 2023 and Rules in 2025"],
  ["A fresh draft followed, and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 finally received Parliamentary passage and Presidential assent in August 2023, six years after the Court had said this needed to happen. Even with the Act passed, India's privacy framework remained largely theoretical until the Digital Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025 were notified, establishing the Data Protection Board and setting out the operational details on a phased timeline running into 2027. So was privacy there? In one sense, the Supreme Court said it always had been. Whether it is meaningfully there now depends on how consistently the Board and the businesses they govern actually behave over the years ahead.", "A new draft followed. Parliament passed the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, and the President approved it in August 2023. This was six years after the Court said a law was needed. The system still existed mostly on paper until the Digital Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025 were issued. The Rules created the Data Protection Board and set practical steps that start in stages through 2027. The Supreme Court said the right to privacy had always existed. Whether it works in practice now depends on how consistently the Board and regulated businesses act in the coming years."],

  // ---- childrens-data-consent ----
  ["If your product could plausibly have a user under 18, and for most consumer apps it could, the DPDP Act's children's data provisions apply whether or not you built your platform with children in mind. The penalties for getting this wrong are among the steepest in the entire framework.", "If your product could have a user under 18, the DPDP Act's rules for children's data apply. This is true even if the platform was not designed for children. Most consumer apps could have users under 18. The penalties for breaking these rules are among the highest in the framework."],
  ["Section 9(1) requires a Data Fiduciary to obtain verifiable consent from a parent or lawful guardian before processing a child's personal data. Rule 10 sets out how: the Data Fiduciary must apply due diligence to confirm that whoever is identifying themselves as the parent is genuinely an identifiable adult, checking either reliable identity and age details it already holds, or details the parent voluntarily provides, including through identity mechanisms like a Digital Locker. A simple tick-box confirming the parent is over 18, filled in on the parent's behalf, does not meet this bar.", "Section 9(1) requires a Data Fiduciary to get permission that can be checked from a parent or lawful guardian before using a child's personal data. Rule 10 explains the checks. The organisation must take proper care to confirm that the person claiming to be the parent is a real adult whose identity can be confirmed. It may check reliable identity and age details it already has, or details the parent chooses to provide, including through a Digital Locker. A simple box saying the parent is over 18, filled in for the parent, is not enough."],
  ["The absolute ban on tracking", "Tracking children is completely banned"],
  ["Section 9(3) is unconditional in a way few other parts of the Act are. A Data Fiduciary must not undertake tracking or behavioural monitoring of children, or target advertising at them, regardless of whether a parent has consented to it. Section 9(2) adds a further requirement that processing must not cause any detrimental effect on a child's wellbeing. Consent can authorise the processing a service genuinely needs to function. It cannot authorise building an advertising profile of a minor.", "Section 9(3) has no general exception. A Data Fiduciary must not track children, monitor their behaviour, or target ads at them, even if a parent gives permission. Section 9(2) also says that use of the data must not harm a child's wellbeing. Permission can allow use of data that a service truly needs to work. It cannot allow an advertising profile to be built for a child."],
  ["Age-gating in practice", "Checking age in practice"],
  ["Product teams need to build these constraints directly into the user onboarding flow.", "Product teams must build these rules directly into the signup process."],
  ["Implement real age-gating at signup, rather than a single self-declared checkbox that anyone can click past.", "Use a real age check at signup, not one box that anyone can tick and pass."],
  ["Build a parental verification flow that checks an identifiable adult, not just a click, before unlocking the account.", "Before opening the account, use a parent check that confirms a real adult's identity, not just a click."],
  ["Ensure analytics and advertising SDKs are hardcoded to default off for any account flagged as belonging to a minor.", "Make analytics and advertising SDKs stay off by default for every account marked as belonging to a child."],
  ["Run periodic audits of third-party SDKs to confirm none of them are quietly profiling or targeting minor accounts behind the scenes.", "Regularly audit outside SDKs to make sure none secretly profile or target children's accounts."],
  ["The bar here is deliberately high. With penalties running up to two hundred crore rupees for violations of these specific obligations, the law's seriousness is calibrated to match the population least able to consent for itself.", "These rules are intentionally strict. Breaking these specific duties can bring penalties of up to two hundred crore rupees. The law sets this high level because children are least able to give permission for themselves."],
];
